-- sus.rofl -- SUS: suspicion under semirings.
--
-- A social-deduction table (Mafia, Among Us, Blood on the Clocktower). The
-- engine does not play. It explains.
--
-- Why this domain is not an illustration of the perspective model but an
-- instance of it:
--
--   1. Every player holds their own, necessarily incomplete, book of what
--      they saw. That book is a LEDGER, in the sense of
--      docs/choosing-perspectives.md: `said[pink](...)` is what pink holds
--      true, `authority(pink, pink)` is the whole list of who may write
--      there, and an entry written by anyone else surfaces as
--      `forged[audit]` without a line of enforcement code.
--   2. Players make CLAIMS, not reports of fact. A claim may be false by
--      construction -- that is what a traitor is for -- so nothing here may
--      read a claim as data.
--   3. Contradicting claims coexist LEGALLY. `said[pink](k5, saw(green,
--      electrical), 2, 2)` and `said[green](k7, saw(green, cafeteria), 2, 2)`
--      are different facts because their perspectives differ, and the
--      contradiction between them is the main signal rather than a data
--      error to be resolved.
--
-- The one bridging assumption the whole model rests on, stated once:
-- CREW NEVER LIE, TRAITORS MAY. Everything below is a consequence.
--
-- The metric is model counting. A world is an assignment of the two traitor
-- roles; C(8,2) = 28 of them; a world is consistent when no live claim, read
-- under that assumption, is false in it. The share of consistent worlds in
-- which a player is a traitor is the suspicion, and it is what people at the
-- table compute by eye and badly.
--
-- Data is synthetic and hand-built (see README.md). The transcript is a
-- game that never happened, arranged so that the engine accuses in round 3
-- and withdraws in round 5.

-- =========================================================================
-- 0. who may write where
-- =========================================================================
-- Two source ledgers per the discipline: `[public]`, the game log, written
-- by the moderator; and one per player, written by that player alone. Two
-- derived ledgers: `[worlds]`, the book of the world enumeration, and
-- `[case]`, the book of the inquiry. Their writers are rules, and every
-- rule that crosses from a claim into a conclusion says so by naming its
-- head perspective -- which is what emits `bridge_decl` and keeps
-- `leak[audit]` empty.

authority(main, moderator).
authority(public, moderator).
authority(red, red).
authority(blue, blue).
authority(green, green).
authority(pink, pink).
authority(cyan, cyan).
authority(lime, lime).
authority(white, white).
authority(black, black).

-- [case] is where testimony from every speaker is collected: `claim[case](P,
-- K, C, A, T) :- said[P](K, C, A, T)` and its three siblings read a speaker's
-- own book under the perspective VARIABLE [P]. That is a crossing `imports`
-- cannot license -- `$var("P")` is not a registered perspective and cannot be
-- the From of an import -- and it is entirely deliberate: collecting what
-- each player said, with the speaker relocated from the perspective slot into
-- an ordinary argument, is what the case file IS. Declared once here, at the
-- ledger that gathers; the hops out of [case] into [worlds] stay subject to
-- the ordinary audit, and an undeclared one would still be a leak.
collects(case).

-- THREE MORE, and the reason they were never typed: `crossing` used to carry
-- `not bridge_decl(R, A, B)`, a row the kernel emitted for any rule whose head
-- named a ledger and whose body read another. Every named crossing here
-- licensed itself by existing; only the gather above, which the kernel could
-- not emit for, ever had to be declared. These three report now.
--
-- [case] READS [worlds] and [worlds] READS [case]. The two books are read into
-- each other on purpose: a world is a hypothesis ABOUT the case file, and the
-- case file records which worlds survive. `imports` is not symmetric, so both
-- directions are separate sentences and both are true here.
imports(case, worlds).
imports(worlds, case).

-- [worlds] READS [public], the moderator's game log. A hypothesis about who
-- lied has to read what was said in the open.
imports(worlds, public).

-- `imports(case, public)` is NOT written, and that is a measurement rather
-- than an omission: `sees` is the transitive closure of `imports`, so
-- `imports(case, worlds)` and `imports(worlds, public)` already give
-- `sees(case, public)`. Adding it changes no row. A declaration that licenses
-- nothing already licensed is a decoration.


-- =========================================================================
-- 1. the game log -- [public], written by the moderator
-- =========================================================================
-- The index on `player` exists because the kernel's comparison builtins are
-- arithmetic: `A < B` over atoms binds nothing, so the unordered pair that
-- makes a world needs a number to break the symmetry.

player[public](red,   1) @init.
player[public](blue,  2) @init.
player[public](green, 3) @init.
player[public](pink,  4) @init.
player[public](cyan,  5) @init.
player[public](lime,  6) @init.
player[public](white, 7) @init.
player[public](black, 8) @init.

traitors[public](2) @init.
horizon[public](6)  @init.
now[public](1)      @init.

-- bodies: victim, room, the round during which they died
kill[public](white, electrical, 2) @init.
kill[public](black, storage,    4) @init.

-- the table voted green out in round 3, on the engine's own accusation.
-- No role card was shown, so this constrains nothing -- it is a DECISION,
-- and section 7 marks it at risk when its support collapses.
ejected[public](green, 3) @init.

-- round 5: pink is caught on camera and ejected, and the card IS shown.
-- This is the revelation. It is a fact about roles, and it prunes worlds.
exposed[public](pink, traitor, 5) @init.

-- round 4: red admits the round-3 sighting was a mistake and takes it back.
withdrawn[public](k10, 4) @init.

-- the claims, numbered, so that a pair of them has an order and the
-- likelihood scan in section 8 has something to iterate over
claim_no[public](k1,   1) @init.  claim_no[public](k2,   2) @init.
claim_no[public](k3,   3) @init.  claim_no[public](k4,   4) @init.
claim_no[public](k5,   5) @init.  claim_no[public](k6,   6) @init.
claim_no[public](k7,   7) @init.  claim_no[public](k8,   8) @init.
claim_no[public](k9,   9) @init.  claim_no[public](k10, 10) @init.
claim_no[public](k11, 11) @init.  claim_no[public](k12, 12) @init.
claim_no[public](k13, 13) @init.  claim_no[public](k14, 14) @init.
claim_no[public](k15, 15) @init.  claim_no[public](k16, 16) @init.
last_claim[public](16) @init.

-- how boldly each player lies WHEN THEY ARE A TRAITOR, in percent. A prior
-- on people, not on claims; it never touches the Boolean answer and only
-- ranks the worlds the Boolean answer already admits (section 8).
deceit[public](red,   50) @init.
deceit[public](blue,  50) @init.
deceit[public](green, 50) @init.
deceit[public](pink,  85) @init.
deceit[public](cyan,  60) @init.
deceit[public](lime,  35) @init.
deceit[public](white, 50) @init.
deceit[public](black, 50) @init.

-- =========================================================================
-- 2. the claims -- one ledger per player
-- =========================================================================
-- `said[P](Id, Content, About, At)`: player P entered claim Id in their own
-- book at round At, about round About. The two rounds are different columns
-- on purpose: k9 is made in round 3 about round 2, and "what was knowable at
-- round 3" needs the round it was SAID, while "what it asserts" needs the
-- round it is ABOUT.
--
-- Four contents, and one of them is deliberately worthless:
--   saw(Q, Room)  -- Q was in Room. Constrains only by contradicting another
--                    sighting: nobody is in two rooms at once.
--   vouch(Q)      -- I watched Q complete a visual task. Traitors cannot, so
--                    a truthful vouch makes Q crew.
--   accuse(Q)     -- I saw Q kill or vent. A truthful accusation makes Q a
--                    traitor.
--   alone         -- I was doing tasks by myself. UNFALSIFIABLE: no rule
--                    below consumes it, so it refutes no world and appears
--                    in no explanation. Real tables are full of these.
--
-- The `-- @who X` markers are not kernel syntax; they are comments that
-- examples/sus/demo.ts splits on, so that each player's entries are loaded
-- under that player's identity. That is the whole forgery story: WHO wrote a
-- fact is the load identity checked against `authority`, never a column.

-- @who white
said[white](k1, saw(white, electrical), 1, 1) @init.

-- @who red
said[red](k2, alone, 1, 1) @init.
said[red](k10, saw(green, electrical), 2, 3) @init.

-- @who blue
said[blue](k8, alone, 2, 2) @init.

-- @who green
said[green](k4, saw(green, medbay), 1, 1) @init.
said[green](k7, saw(green, cafeteria), 2, 2) @init.
said[green](k12, accuse(pink), 2, 3) @init.

-- @who pink
said[pink](k5, saw(green, electrical), 2, 2) @init.
said[pink](k9, accuse(green), 2, 3) @init.

-- @who cyan
said[cyan](k6, saw(green, electrical), 2, 3) @init.
said[cyan](k13, alone, 4, 4) @init.
said[cyan](k15, saw(cyan, reactor), 4, 6) @init.

-- @who lime
said[lime](k3, vouch(red), 1, 1) @init.
said[lime](k14, saw(cyan, storage), 4, 6) @init.
said[lime](k16, accuse(cyan), 4, 6) @init.

-- @who black
said[black](k11, saw(black, storage), 3, 3) @init.

-- @who moderator
-- =========================================================================
-- 3. time
-- =========================================================================
-- Rounds are ticks, and persistence is not a storage property: the ledgers
-- survive a tick only because these rules carry them. One carry rule per
-- player ledger, written out rather than expressed once with a perspective
-- variable -- see README.md: `said[P](..) @next :- said[P](..)` reads and
-- writes the same variable perspective, the audit sees only `$any` on both
-- ends, and `leak[audit]($any, $any)` fires. Naming the ledger is the fix
-- and it is also the more honest statement: each book carries itself.

now[public](T1)          @next :- now[public](T), horizon[public](H), T < H, T1 is T + 1.
now[public](T)           @next :- now[public](T), horizon[public](H), T >= H.
horizon[public](H)       @next :- horizon[public](H).
traitors[public](N)      @next :- traitors[public](N).
last_claim[public](L)    @next :- last_claim[public](L).
player[public](P, I)     @next :- player[public](P, I).
kill[public](V, R, T)    @next :- kill[public](V, R, T).
ejected[public](P, T)    @next :- ejected[public](P, T).
exposed[public](P, R, T) @next :- exposed[public](P, R, T).
withdrawn[public](K, T)  @next :- withdrawn[public](K, T).
claim_no[public](K, N)   @next :- claim_no[public](K, N).
deceit[public](P, D)     @next :- deceit[public](P, D).

said[red](K, C, A, T)    @next :- said[red](K, C, A, T).
said[blue](K, C, A, T)   @next :- said[blue](K, C, A, T).
said[green](K, C, A, T)  @next :- said[green](K, C, A, T).
said[pink](K, C, A, T)   @next :- said[pink](K, C, A, T).
said[cyan](K, C, A, T)   @next :- said[cyan](K, C, A, T).
said[lime](K, C, A, T)   @next :- said[lime](K, C, A, T).
said[white](K, C, A, T)  @next :- said[white](K, C, A, T).
said[black](K, C, A, T)  @next :- said[black](K, C, A, T).

-- =========================================================================
-- 4. the inquiry's index -- [case]
-- =========================================================================
-- Every rule here reads a player's book and writes the inquiry's. That
-- crossing is the point: the claim stays in the claimant's ledger, and what
-- the inquiry knows about it is a separate entry with the claimant named.

claim[case](K, P, About, At)         :- said[P](K, _, About, At).
claimant[case](K, P)                 :- claim[case](K, P, _, _).
sighting[case](K, P, Q, Room, About) :- said[P](K, saw(Q, Room), About, _).
vouching[case](K, P, Q, About)       :- said[P](K, vouch(Q), About, _).
charge[case](K, P, Q, About)         :- said[P](K, accuse(Q), About, _).

-- STATED vs LIVE. A claim is stated once it has been made; it stays stated
-- for ever, because the table heard it. It is live while it still carries
-- weight. Annulment is the difference, and the two annulments are not the
-- same arithmetic (section 7).
stated[case](K)              :- claim[case](K, _, _, At), now[public](N), At <= N.
annulled[case](K, withdrawn) :- withdrawn[public](K, TW), now[public](N), TW <= N.
annulled[case](K, unmasked)  :- claimant[case](K, P), exposed[public](P, traitor, TE),
                                now[public](N), TE <= N.
live[case](K)                :- stated[case](K), not annulled[case](K, _).

-- Two sightings that put the same player in two rooms in the same round.
-- Both may stand; at most one may be true. `clash` is the structural fact
-- and survives annulment, `conflict` is the live one that refutes worlds.
clash[case](K1, K2)    :- sighting[case](K1, _, Q, R1, T), sighting[case](K2, _, Q, R2, T),
                          R1 != R2, claim_no[public](K1, N1), claim_no[public](K2, N2),
                          N1 < N2, stated[case](K1), stated[case](K2).
conflict[case](K1, K2) :- clash[case](K1, K2), live[case](K1), live[case](K2).

dead[case](P)  :- kill[public](P, _, T), now[public](N), T <= N.
dead[case](P)  :- ejected[public](P, T), now[public](N), T <= N.
dead[case](P)  :- exposed[public](P, _, T), now[public](N), T <= N.

-- =========================================================================
-- 5. the worlds -- [worlds]
-- =========================================================================
-- A world names the two traitors. Nothing else about the world is modelled:
-- who was where is not a hidden variable here, it is only ever a claim.

world[worlds](w(A, B))         :- player[public](A, I), player[public](B, J), I < J.
traitor_in[worlds](w(A, B), A) :- world[worlds](w(A, B)).
traitor_in[worlds](w(A, B), B) :- world[worlds](w(A, B)).
crew_in[worlds](W, P)          :- world[worlds](W), player[public](P, _),
                                  not traitor_in[worlds](W, P).

-- REFUTATION. Every clause below names the reason as a term, so that the
-- refutation is attributable rather than a bare Boolean: a claim id when a
-- single claim does it, a term when the game log or a pair of claims does.

-- a traitor is not murdered by their own side
refuted_by[worlds](W, victim(V)) :- kill[public](V, _, T), now[public](N), T <= N,
                                    traitor_in[worlds](W, V).

-- a revealed card is not a claim: it is the log, and it is binding
refuted_by[worlds](W, reveal(P)) :- exposed[public](P, traitor, T), now[public](N), T <= N,
                                    crew_in[worlds](W, P).
refuted_by[worlds](W, reveal(P)) :- exposed[public](P, crew, T), now[public](N), T <= N,
                                    traitor_in[worlds](W, P).

-- a crew member never lies, so a crew accusation of a crewmate cannot stand
refuted_by[worlds](W, K) :- charge[case](K, P, Q, _), live[case](K),
                            crew_in[worlds](W, P), crew_in[worlds](W, Q).

-- ... nor a crew vouch for a traitor
refuted_by[worlds](W, K) :- vouching[case](K, P, Q, _), live[case](K),
                            crew_in[worlds](W, P), traitor_in[worlds](W, Q).

-- ... nor two crew putting the same player in two rooms at once
refuted_by[worlds](W, clash(K1, K2)) :- conflict[case](K1, K2),
                                        claimant[case](K1, P1), claimant[case](K2, P2),
                                        crew_in[worlds](W, P1), crew_in[worlds](W, P2).

-- the victim was in the room they died in
refuted_by[worlds](W, K) :- kill[public](V, Room, T), now[public](N), T <= N,
                            sighting[case](K, P, V, R2, T), R2 != Room,
                            live[case](K), crew_in[worlds](W, P).

-- and somebody was there to do it: if every traitor of W is truthfully
-- placed somewhere else at that moment, nobody in W could have killed
elsewhere[worlds](W, Q, Room, T) :- kill[public](_, Room, T),
                                    sighting[case](K, P, Q, R2, T), R2 != Room,
                                    live[case](K), crew_in[worlds](W, P).
refuted_by[worlds](W, nobody_there(Room, T)) :-
        kill[public](_, Room, T), now[public](N), T <= N,
        traitor_in[worlds](W, X), traitor_in[worlds](W, Y), X != Y,
        elsewhere[worlds](W, X, Room, T), elsewhere[worlds](W, Y, Room, T).

impossible[worlds](W)   :- refuted_by[worlds](W, _).
consistent[worlds](W)   :- world[worlds](W), not impossible[worlds](W).
guilty_in[worlds](P, W) :- consistent[worlds](W), traitor_in[worlds](W, P).

-- THE COUNT IS THE METRIC. `outcome(guilty, P)` has exactly one derivation
-- per consistent world in which P is a traitor; `outcome(any, P)` has one
-- per consistent world. The counting semiring folded over the support the
-- engine already recorded returns both numbers, and their ratio is the
-- suspicion. Nothing is enumerated in TypeScript to get it.
outcome[case](guilty, P) :- guilty_in[worlds](P, W).
outcome[case](any, P)    :- consistent[worlds](W), player[public](P, _).

-- =========================================================================
-- 6. what clears you, and the price of an accusation
-- =========================================================================
-- The two questions a player actually asks, and they are the same relation
-- read from two sides: which reasons rule out the worlds where I am guilty,
-- and which rule out the worlds where I am innocent.

-- `price(P, W, R)`: W would make P a traitor, and R is what rules W out. It
-- is simultaneously the answer to "what clears P" and to "what would you have
-- to declare false to accuse P anyway", which is the same set read from the
-- two ends.
price[case](P, W, R)        :- world[worlds](W), traitor_in[worlds](W, P),
                               refuted_by[worlds](W, R).
-- and the mirror: W would make P innocent, and R is what rules W out.
incriminates[case](P, W, R) :- world[worlds](W), crew_in[worlds](W, P),
                               refuted_by[worlds](W, R).

-- =========================================================================
-- 7. annulment, and what it takes with it
-- =========================================================================
-- `points_at(Q, K)`: claim K puts Q in the frame -- an accusation of Q, or
-- somebody else's sighting of Q that Q's own account contradicts. Built on
-- `clash` and `stated`, NOT on `conflict` and `live`, because the question
-- here is what the case against Q was BUILT on, and annulling a claim must
-- not quietly erase the fact that the case ever rested on it.

points_at[case](Q, K) :- charge[case](K, _, Q, _), stated[case](K).
points_at[case](Q, K) :- clash[case](K, K2), sighting[case](K, P, Q, _, _), P != Q,
                         sighting[case](K2, Q, Q, _, _).
points_at[case](Q, K) :- clash[case](K2, K), sighting[case](K, P, Q, _, _), P != Q,
                         sighting[case](K2, Q, Q, _, _).

-- The support graph of the conclusions the table acted on. Each accuser's
-- case is its own conclusion, which is what lets the engine say WHICH LINK
-- broke instead of only that something did.
case_against[case](Q, P)                          :- points_at[case](Q, K), claimant[case](K, P).
rests_on[case](case_against(Q, P), K)             :- points_at[case](Q, K), claimant[case](K, P).
rests_on[case](finding(Q), case_against(Q, P))    :- case_against[case](Q, P).
rests_on[case](ejection(Q), finding(Q))           :- ejected[public](Q, _), case_against[case](Q, _).
rests_on[case](X, Z)                              :- rests_on[case](X, Y), rests_on[case](Y, Z).

leaf[case](X, K)  :- rests_on[case](X, K), claim[case](K, _, _, _).
gone[case](X)     :- leaf[case](X, K), annulled[case](K, _).
standing[case](X) :- leaf[case](X, K), not annulled[case](K, _).
at_risk[case](X)  :- gone[case](X), not standing[case](X).
shaken[case](X)   :- gone[case](X), standing[case](X).

-- =========================================================================
-- 8. which world is most likely -- the deceit priors
-- =========================================================================
-- A world says who the traitors are; it does not say which of their claims
-- were lies. `lie(W, K)` is the part that IS determined: a claim by a
-- traitor of W that some crew member of W contradicts, or that asserts a
-- role W denies. `stance` then classifies every claim in every consistent
-- world into exactly one of four boxes, and the Viterbi fold weights them:
--
--   forced   the claimant is crew in W, so the claim is true by the crew
--            axiom. Carries no information, weight 1.
--   lied     the claimant is a traitor in W and the claim is false there.
--            Weight deceit(P).
--   spared   the claimant is a traitor in W and the claim survives.
--            Weight 1 - deceit(P).
--   pending  not yet said, or annulled. Weight 1.
--
-- The independence assumption is stated rather than hidden: the score is a
-- product over claims, so it treats each claim's honesty as independent
-- given the roles, which is false of any real table where liars coordinate.

lie[worlds](W, K) :- charge[case](K, P, Q, _), live[case](K),
                     traitor_in[worlds](W, P), crew_in[worlds](W, Q).
lie[worlds](W, K) :- vouching[case](K, P, Q, _), live[case](K),
                     traitor_in[worlds](W, P), traitor_in[worlds](W, Q).
lie[worlds](W, K) :- conflict[case](K, K2), claimant[case](K, P1), claimant[case](K2, P2),
                     traitor_in[worlds](W, P1), crew_in[worlds](W, P2).
lie[worlds](W, K) :- conflict[case](K2, K), claimant[case](K, P1), claimant[case](K2, P2),
                     traitor_in[worlds](W, P1), crew_in[worlds](W, P2).

stance[worlds](W, K, forced)  :- consistent[worlds](W), claimant[case](K, P), live[case](K),
                                 crew_in[worlds](W, P).
stance[worlds](W, K, lied)    :- consistent[worlds](W), lie[worlds](W, K).
stance[worlds](W, K, spared)  :- consistent[worlds](W), claimant[case](K, P), live[case](K),
                                 traitor_in[worlds](W, P), not lie[worlds](W, K).
-- keyed on the NUMBERING rather than on the claim, so that excising a claim
-- leaves the scan intact instead of silently truncating every world's score
stance[worlds](W, K, pending) :- consistent[worlds](W), claim_no[public](K, _),
                                 not live[case](K).

-- One derivation per world, visiting every claim exactly once in claim_no
-- order, so the Viterbi product over that derivation is the world's score.
scanned[worlds](W, 0) :- consistent[worlds](W).
scanned[worlds](W, N) :- scanned[worlds](W, M), N is M + 1,
                         claim_no[public](K, N), stance[worlds](W, K, _).
weighed[worlds](W)    :- scanned[worlds](W, L), last_claim[public](L).

-- Acceptance scenario (examples/sus/demo.ts runs all of it):
--   ? consistent[worlds](W)        -> 5 of 28 at round 3, 2 at round 5
--   sus --explain green            -> 5/5 at round 3, 1/2 at round 5
--   sus -n green   (round 6)       -> impossible, and the price of saying
--                                     otherwise, claim by claim
--   which link broke               -> at_risk(case_against(green, pink))
--   oracle                         -> 28 worlds x 6 rounds, enumerated twice
